DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       ssyynncc()  causes  all  pending  modifications  to filesystem metadata and
       cached file data to be written to the underlying filesystems.

       ssyynnccffss() is like ssyynncc(), but synchronizes just the filesystem  contain‐
       ing file referred to by the open file descriptor _f_d.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       ssyynnccffss()  returns  0 on success; on error, it returns -1 and sets _e_r_r_n_o
       to indicate the error.

EERRRROORRSS
       ssyynncc() is always successful.

       ssyynnccffss() can fail for at least the following reasons:

       EEBBAADDFF  _f_d is not a valid file descriptor.

       EEIIOO    An error occurred during synchronization.  This error may relate
              to data written to any file on the filesystem,  or  on  metadata
              related to the filesystem itself.

       EENNOOSSPPCC Disk space was exhausted while synchronizing.

       EENNOOSSPPCC
       EEDDQQUUOOTT Data  was  written  to a file on NFS or another filesystem which
              does not allocate space at the time of a wwrriittee(2)  system  call,
              and  some  previous  write  failed  due  to insufficient storage
              space.

VVEERRSSIIOONNSS
       According to the standard specification (i.e., POSIX.1), ssyynncc()  sched‐
       ules  the  writes,  but  may  return before the actual writing is done.
       However Linux waits for I/O completions, and thus  ssyynncc()  or  ssyynnccffss()
       provide the same guarantees as ffssyynncc() called on every file in the sys‐
       tem or filesystem respectively.

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
       ssyynncc() POSIX.1‐2024.

       ssyynnccffss()
              Linux.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
       ssyynncc() POSIX.1‐2001, SVr4, 4.3BSD.

       ssyynnccffss()
              Linux 2.6.39, glibc 2.14.

       Since  glibc  2.2.2, the Linux prototype for ssyynncc() is as listed above,
       following the various standards.  In glibc 2.2.1 and  earlier,  it  was
       "int sync(void)", and ssyynncc() always returned 0.

       In mainline kernel versions prior to Linux 5.8, ssyynnccffss() will fail only
       when  passed  a bad file descriptor (EEBBAADDFF).  Since Linux 5.8, ssyynnccffss()
       will also report an error if one or more inodes failed  to  be  written
       back since the last ssyynnccffss() call.

BBUUGGSS
       Before  Linux 1.3.20, Linux did not wait for I/O to complete before re‐
       turning.

